    "agent_last_message": "The tool is in `output/`. Build it from inside that directory with the spec's command:\n\n```\ncd output && cmake -B build && cmake --build build\n./build/wordcount --input corpus.txt --output result.json\n```\n\nFiles: `CMakeLists.txt`, `src/main.cpp` (flags and exit codes), and `src/wordcount.hpp`/`wordcount.cpp` (streaming analysis and JSON). It builds with no warnings under `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic` on GCC 13 and 14. The default build type is Release.\n\n**Choices the spec left open.** The first one is the most likely to matter.\n- **\"Malformed\" is undefined.** I treat a file containing a NUL byte as not plain text and exit 1. Other bytes, including invalid UTF-8, are counted byte by byte as the spec says.\n- **Lowercasing is ASCII-only**, since characters are bytes. `CAF\u00c9` and `caf\u00e9` count as different words.\n- **Non-ASCII words in the JSON.** Valid UTF-8 is written unchanged. Any other byte is written as `\\u00XX`, so the output is always valid JSON.\n- **Output errors exit 1.** An unwritable `--output` path is something the user can fix. The file is written only after analysis succeeds, so a failed run leaves no partial output.\n- **Arguments.** `--input x` and `--input=x` both work. Duplicate flags, positional arguments, and `--help` exit 1, since the spec defines only the two flags.\n\n**Verification.** The test scripts are in `/tmp/wc-tests`, not in `output/`.\n- 190 checks, with expected values worked out by hand from the spec. They cover every edge case, the ordering, the top-10 limit, the JSON format, and every exit-code path.\n- 424 words with arbitrary bytes, checked against Python's strict UTF-8 decoder.\n- Random corpora and read-buffer boundary cases, compared against an independent bytes-based reference, all with exact matches.\n- A 420 MB file: line, byte, and word totals match `wc`, and the top 10 match a `sort | uniq` pipeline.\n- ASan and UBSan report nothing. Forcing out-of-memory with a memory cap exits 2.\n\n**Performance.** About 120 MB/s on one core: 420 MB in",
